    {
      "text": "Heterosexuality is a political institution that keeps women subordinate to men, not simply a natural orientation.",
      "value": 2,
      "rationale": "Rich, the Leeds group and the Furies all state it as the movement's central thesis.",
      "sources": [
        "s7",
        "s8",
        "s2"
      ],
      "declined": "Theory wording a general reader would need explained."
    },
    {
      "text": "Women should organise politically in groups that exclude men.",
      "value": 1,
      "rationale": "The Furies, Leeds and Frye argue for women-only and separate organising, while Combahee rejects separatism as a strategy.",
      "sources": [
        "s2",
        "s8",
        "s9",
        "s11"
      ],
      "addedAs": "women-organise-apart-from-men"
    },
    {
      "text": "Feminists should end sexual and emotional relationships with men as a political act.",
      "value": 1,
      "rationale": "Bunch, Brown and the Leeds group argue that feminists should give up relationships with men; Reid and others describe it as an option, and Combahee rejects the separatist version.",
      "sources": [
        "s2",
        "s3",
        "s8",
        "s11"
      ],
      "declined": "A personal choice, not a political position most people could answer."
